%[text] # ME2801: HW1, Part 2: Inverse Laplace by PFE
%%
%[text] Given:
%[text] $F(s) = \\frac{3}{s(s+3)}$
%[text] Find: $f(t) = \\mathcal{L}^{-1} F(s) $
%[text] ## Soln:
%[text] **Express** $F(s)$ as a ratio of polynomials, numerator and denominator
num = 3;
den = [1 3 0];
den = conv([1 0],[1 3]); % Equivalently
F = tf(num,den)  % This is covered in Week 2 %[output:76c84911]
%[text] **Call** [`residue`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/residue.html) to do the arithmetic of PFE.
[r, p, k] = residue(num, den) %[output:01f11443] %[output:36756ba1] %[output:0186dc36]
%[text] **Interpret** the results.  
%[text] - Two real poles at p = -3 and p = 0.  \
%[text] $F(s) = \\frac{\\mathtt{r(1)}}\n{s+\\mathtt{p(1)}}\n+\n \\frac{\\mathtt{r(2)}}\n{s+\\mathtt{p(2)}}\n+ \n\\ldots$
%[text] $F(s) = \\frac{1}{s+3} + \\frac{-1}{s+0}$
%[text] **Apply** inverse Laplace transform pairs and properties.
%[text] $f(t) = \\mathcal{L}^{-1} F(s) = \\mathcal{L}^{-1} \\frac{1}{s+3} - \\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\frac{1}{s}$
%[text] $f(t) = 1 - e^{-3t}$
%%
%[text] Given
%[text] $F(s) = \\frac{10}{s(s^2 + 4s + 8)}$
num = 10;
den = [1 4 8 0];
F = tf(num, den) % Visual check %[output:67d4c4b7]
[K, p, k] = residue(num, den) %[output:6eb5c1eb] %[output:5924f822] %[output:518b2873]
%[text] **Complex roots** - real and imaginary!
%[text] ![](text:image:3ba9)
%[text] Identify:
%[text] - $\\alpha = -0.625$
%[text] - $\\beta = -0.625$
%[text] - $a = 2.0$
%[text] - $\\omega = 2.0$ \
%[text] Don't forget the third root/pole at $p = 0$!
%[text] $f(t) = 2 \[ -0.625 \\, e^{-2t}\\cos(2t) - 0.625 \\,e^{-2t}\\sin(2t)\] + 1.25$
%[text] which we may simplify further (but not required)
%[text] $f(t) = 1.25 \\left\[ 1 -  e^{-2t}(\\cos(2t) +\\sin(2t))\\right\] $
%[text] What does this function look like?
tt = linspace(0,10,1000);
f = 1.25*(1 - exp(-2*tt).*(cos(2*tt) + sin(2*tt)));
plot(tt, f, 'LineWidth', 1.5); %[output:5c902451]
xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Response - f(t) (n/a)'); grid on; %[output:5c902451]
%[text] 

